{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 45, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3.5-122b-a10b", "content": "Raymond III was a prominent nobleman who ruled the County of Tripoli during a turbulent era of the Crusades. His life was defined by complex political maneuvering and constant warfare against Muslim forces in the Holy Land. He inherited his title at a young age and served as regent for his cousin Bohemond III of Antioch before eventually becoming the sole ruler of his own domain. His leadership style often prioritized stability and diplomacy over reckless aggression which sometimes caused friction with other Crusader leaders who favored immediate military action.\n\n*Early Life and Inheritance*\nRaymond was born into the prestigious House of Tripoli around the year 1140 or 1141. His father Raymond II had been assassinated by the Hashashin sect which forced the young boy to assume leadership under the protection of powerful nobles. He traveled to Europe to seek support and manage his estates while learning the intricacies of feudal politics. Upon his return he found that the Kingdom of Jerusalem was in chaos following the death of King Baldwin IV. His position as the most senior prince in the Latin East made him a crucial figure in the succession crisis that plagued the realm.\n\n*Political Struggles and Regency*\nThe Count of Tripoli frequently clashed with Guy of Lusignan who married Sibylla and claimed the throne. Raymond believed Guy was unfit to rule and sought to install Baldwin V as king instead. This disagreement led to a civil war within the Crusader states that weakened their collective defense against Saladin. Raymond eventually reconciled with Guy but only after securing a promise that he would not be excluded from the royal council. His ability to navigate these dangerous waters demonstrated his political acumen even if it earned him the nickname of a traitor by some contemporaries who felt he was too cautious.\n\n*The Battle of Hattin and Death*\nRaymond made the controversial decision to withdraw his forces from the main Crusader army before the Battle of Hattin in 1187. He argued that the location chosen by Guy was strategically unsound and that the army would be trapped. Saladin exploited this disunity and destroyed the Crusader forces which led to the fall of Jerusalem. Raymond himself managed to escape the slaughter and returned to Tripoli to defend his city against the subsequent Muslim offensive. He died in 1187 or 1189 shortly after these events or perhaps due to natural causes before the city fell. His death marked the end of an era for the County of Tripoli which would continue under the rule of his cousin Bohemond III until the final fall of the Crusader states. The legacy of Raymond III remains a subject of historical debate regarding his role in the disaster at Hattin and his overall contribution to the survival of the Latin East.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.003035439}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -1.549722e-06}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -0.002323404}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.087599985}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.010190256}, {"token": " prominent", "logprob": -0.9670736}, {"token": " noble", "logprob": -0.04369542}, {"token": "man", "logprob": -0.10268919}, {"token": " who", "logprob": -0.13099675}, {"token": " ruled", "logprob": -0.67562395}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.08308808}, {"token": " County", "logprob": -0.00044242392}, {"token": " of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -4.768373e-07}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " during", "logprob": -0.0018360174}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -3.7732491}, {"token": " turbulent", "logprob": -0.19662203}, {"token": " era", "logprob": -0.07945398}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -2.417476}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.31080642}, {"token": " Crus", "logprob": -0.06836881}, {"token": "ades", "logprob": -0.0011315189}, {"token": ".", "logprob": -0.0014605455}, {"token": " His", "logpr